11. How I'd turn this research into a durable production system
Rather than creating twelve disconnected research folders, extend your existing porting workflow with a reusable source intake catalogue.

Every prospective work would have:

Source identity: canonical work, specific edition or translation, teacher, language and provenance.

Audio receipt: URL or local file, duration, indexed chapters or verses, voice type, transcript availability and access rights.

Research state: verified recording, text aligned, speaker roles separated, philosophical units excavated.

Film state: emerging questions, locked Essay 0 spine, A/B/C manifests, provenance and seam-audit results.

The source would enter the actual essay-building pipeline only once its audio and text have been matched and its intended use is cleared.

That would make the most valuable existing Vātūlanātha tooling reusable instead of repeatedly starting the excavation process from zero.

Recommended next sequence
My highest-priority three would be Paramārthasāra, Dṛg-Dṛśya Viveka, and Nāgārjuna's Ratnāvalī. They provide three complementary studies of selfhood: Trika recognition, Advaita discrimination, and Buddhist dependent existence.

I'd use Dṛg-Dṛśya Viveka as the next technical pilot because its seven MP3 lessons and supplied text offer a manageable unit of work. I'd keep Paramārthasāra as the flagship Trika project once the original audio can be accessed and publishing permissions resolved. Ratnāvalī would be the most substantial subsequent Buddhist project.

The broader discovery is that this format doesn't have to be restricted to Trika, or even to India. There is enough verified recorded material to build an extensive collection of philosophical enquiries, while preserving the same rigor: the original words, the original argument, an intelligible question, and an ever-deepening encounter with the text.

