Dharma Film System — Visual Engine Architecture Review
Review date: 2026-10-26
Source project: uploaded essay-visual-engine.zip; GitHub repos prx0r/analayo-nibbana, prx0r/shiva-sutras, prx0r/vatulanatha
Purpose: a reusable, faithful, source-verified pipeline for Standard Compilations and Custom enquiry films.

1. Canon / north star
Two outputs share one corpus and one rendering system:

Standard compilation: exact source-text order, every verse or passage, with a chant/reading, faithful translated meaning, archive explanation, and optional elaboration. This is the fidelity object. It cannot silently skip, rewrite, or reorder source material.
Custom compilation: source-grounded, question-led enquiry. Essay 0 = the distilled spine; A = primary answers; B = A with explanatory depth; C = B with complete valuable texture. Layer rules and question sets belong to source projects, not the video renderer.
For both, preserve each provenance tuple (source_id, passage_id, edition_id, file, source_in, source_out, cut_in, cut_out, version, rights). The renderer is downstream of the audio/argument and must never modify text, rewrite quotes, or silently skip recitations.

2. Confirmed current-engine condition (code read + preview)
Uploaded ZIP is a ~1.5 MB Python/Pillow/NumPy/OpenCV/FFmpeg compositor. ve/render.py reads timeline.json, builds a single master audio, composites animated left cards, a right art playlist, and ve/fluid.py's colored spectral waveform. A 60s synthetic demo timeline was generated; validation passed; stills were rendered with right.mode=etch at 12s, 20s, 32s, 48s. This verifies the preview path, not the full engine, Sleepdraw integration, or completed-video export.

Good implementation choices: deterministic timeline/voice mapping, right-art independent clock, no reliance on speaker diarization, Chinese/Devanagari-friendly font provision in principle, reuse of source project manifests, caching of pre-rendered artwork, and source-linked beat IDs.

Defects / blockers to fix:

No subtitle rail: current bottom region is a role-colored waveform, not captions. Actual word- or phrase-timed captions are not in the schema/render pass.
Verse disappears during explanatory quote: ve/cards.py draws the newest card only. On archive playback, an explanation quote replaces the original verse; the most important primary text is lost.
Fake pen activity in default Etch: ve/right.py ranks image pixels by edge strength and pseudorandom low-frequency masks. This is a pixel reveal, not vector pen strokes or deliberate anatomical drawing. sleepdraw mode delegates to an external repo, and was not tested.
Risky demo setup: documented python -m ve.cli demo defaults to sleepdraw but requires a working SLEEPDRAW_DIR, repo code and vendor dependencies. Provide an out-of-box built-in etch demo and label it preview-quality.
No meaningfully typed visual assets: adapters/shiva_sutras.py repeats provided images every ~5 min, independent of exact verse, deities or motifs. There is no source-to-iconography mapping.
Weak quote guard: the adapter warns when a verbatim quote is missing but falls back to the authored beat title as a quote card. A missing sourced quotation must instead fail or show no quotation.
Fixed 1920x1080 coordinate assumptions: the artwork stage starts at x=820 and text box extends to x=900. Any new mobile variant or ratio needs coordinated text-safe-zone layout, not a resized 16:9 frame.
No speaker-caption alignment contract: segmented excerpt windows must transform original SRT/VTT or word-timed cues into cut-local and then film-local time. Role identity comes from the edit manifest, not inferred diarization.
Potential font risks: current fonts are adequate for the demo, but Tibetan, Sanskrit conjuncts and multilingual scripts need robust shaping, font tests, overflow detection and print-edition proofing.
Heavy compositor: always recomputing full 1920x1080 frames for long films is costly. Cache right-hand art renders and changed cards as intermediate alpha streams and preserve final FFmpeg muxing.
3. Recommended default screen (1920x1080)
Black canvas, no rectangular visual panels, no artificial border between left and right.

Left field x=110–850: a small section_id/passage_id, original text if compact, English translation or independently sourced English wording. Treat the verse as a persistent anchor, even while teachers' subtitles change. Mark translation provenance in metadata, not noisy on-screen citations.
Right field x=900–1850: hand-drawn sacred illustration or doctrinal diagram, feathered only at the margin. Slow, deliberate construction; do not animate all the time. A finished image can hold for minutes.
Bottom strip y≈860–1030: max two subtitle lines; coloured speaker name or 3-pixel vertical rule; actual speech text in off-white; optional tiny spectrogram only when the piece intentionally demands it.
No fixed Sanskrit + IAST + English + plain English stack: when a verse is short, show script + English; put transliteration in a tiny optional second line, or in captions/metadata. For long verses, default to the meaningful English excerpt and compact script citation, with original script featured at the beat head. For recitations, restore original script/IAST with timed line emphasis.
Readability overrides decorative density. Especially in mobile-safe exports, never render all three linguistic views as equally prominent.

Persistent text rules
verse_anchor lives for an entire source-passage unit and is not owned by a single left-card segment. caption is a separate, independently timed overlay. question temporarily replaces verse during enquiry/bell; primary_text returns at the source unit. translation must be visibly distinct from archival quotations or editorial interpretation.

4. The art: three layers, not one generative mystery
A. Iconographic authority. Build iconography/<figure>.json with tradition, exact form, required hand count, implements, mudras, attributes, pose, permitted variants, historical reference images and rights. Trika: Bhairava, Bhairavī, Kālī in textually appropriate forms; Dzogchen/Vajrayāna: avoid restricted deity iconography or initiation-only practices without explicit checks. The deities are not interchangeable, and there should be no generic cross-tradition slideshow.

B. Pen path / semantics. Produce high-quality B&W master art first; then split into named layers (silhouette, body, face, hands, attributes, background, hatch). Convert line work to vector centerlines. Order draw strokes anatomically (gestures/major silhouette first, details later), preserve the final artwork exactly. Optimize pen-up paths within semantic groups but never globally at the cost of logic. Art storyboard = draw-plan.json with strokes, layer order, duration, image provenance and editable masks.

C. Pixel fidelity / finishing. Cache SVG or stroke sequence at 2K/4K and render to RGBA/alpha once. Optionally add precisely bounded charcoal texture or fixed-image pencil grain. Avoid randomized noise reveals and uncontrolled generative image-to-video as the default. Test first, mid, 95%, and final frames against a locked asset. End frame of a drawing is exactly the approved final reference.

Timing insight
Artwork timing should be partly independent of dialogue edits. A painting may span a whole question, reaching meaningful intermediate states at the Essay A/B/C thresholds. However, the picture must not imply doctrinal claims not present in the source. Negative space is a genuine visual asset; do not fill every silence with deities. For Nibbāna, preserve the locked black/silence law and cessation motifs instead of imposing Trika imagery.

