7. Exactly what to test next
Current renderer baseline: inspect current sample PNGs and create a 30-second audio-aligned preview using a sourced Śiva Sūtras unit. Ensure no quote fallback and no mixed provenance.
Verse persistence: pin the verse above a rotating explanation line. Ensure long-source language text fits with correct shaping, including Devanagari/IAST and optional Tibetan.
Subtitle route A: implement ASS via pysubs2, transformed from the original word-aligned transcript using the cutplan. Use single-color-by-role, not multicolor karaoke distractions.
Stroke A/B test: same final illustration processed through uploaded Etch vs whiteboard-video-engine, both 15 seconds. Assert subject proportions and final artwork pixel match; manually inspect stroke order. Keep source right-panel pipeline compatible with externally rendered RGBA MP4.
Motif rules: add metadata to each primary passage linking it to a doctrinal drawing or none. Make sacred imagery historically and textually grounded; no randomly cycling stock deities.
Tier recursion: choose one visual motif and prove that 0→A→B→C progressively enriches understanding without randomly resetting or inventing new doctrine.
Audit: verify print text hash, captions source ownership, source audio sample alignment, long-verse overflow, subtitle safe area, silence handling, art start/end holds, and frame audio sync.
Ship-quality gates
100% verses have source edition and proofing status; printed original never autogenerated from speech recognition.
100% archive quotations track to file+time; missing quote MUST fail or suppress the quote UI.
Each caption word/line maps onto the cut audio time; speaker identity from the manifest.
No subtitle clashes with an anchored verse or iconographic art.
No unsafe made-up sacred attributes, extra hands, wrong ritual implements, or inappropriate tradition crossover.
Every art piece can be previewed without its audio, with stable and anatomically plausible progression.
Stage animation may run at 25–30fps but has a deterministic frame-to-state map to guarantee reproducibility.
Final film has independently playable subtitle tracks where platform supports them, plus optional burned-in subtitle master.
8. One governing aesthetic rule
TEXT is evidence; AUDIO is transmission; DRAWING is attention.

The rendering system should never permit the third to obscure either of the first two. The artwork should occasionally do nothing and let the viewer read and listen; that restraint is part of the style, not a missing animation.

